  "body": "The recommened stylesheet in [The CSS user agent style sheet and presentational hints](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#bidi-rendering) for `<div>` is a clear indication that `div` _does_ create an additional level of independent text content:\r\n\r\n```\r\naddress, blockquote, center, div, figure, figcaption, footer, form, header, hr,\r\nlegend, listing, main, p, plaintext, pre, summary, xmp, article, aside, h1, h2,\r\nh3, h4, h5, h6, hgroup, nav, section, table, caption, colgroup, col, thead,\r\ntbody, tfoot, tr, td, th, dir, dd, dl, dt, menu, ol, ul, li, bdi, output,\r\n[dir=ltr i], [dir=rtl i], [dir=auto i] {\r\n  unicode-bidi: isolate; \r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nSince the recommened `unicode-bidi` value for a `div` is `isolate`, but the spec also says \"The div element has no special meaning at all\". it's not reasonable that `div`'s semantics is inconsistent with its recommended rendering.\r\n\r\nSo the spec needs to either change the recommend of `unicode-bidi` value for a `div` to `normal`, or rectificate the statement \"The div element has no special meaning at all\" to something like \"The div element has no special meaning at all, except that it semantically creates an additional level of independent text content\", to make semantics and rendering for `div` consistent.\r\n\r\nSince changing the recommend `unicode-bidi` value for a `div` to `normal` would break the web compatibility. So the only reasonable thing to do is to rectificate the statement \"The div element has no special meaning at all\".",
